title: Does typespec allow negative lookaheads in name validation?

question: Hi Team, 
Does typespec allow negative lookahead for validating names? If not can you share the limitations? 
Here is the pattern we are trying to implement:
- Length - 4 - 64
- Alphanumerics and hyphens
- Can't end with period. Start with a letter
- Can't start or end with hyphen. Can't us consecutive hyphens
- Can have spaces but not at the start or end of a name.
```
model StorageDiscoveryScope {
  @doc("Display name of the collection")
  @pattern("^(?! )[a-zA-Z0-9]+(?:[ -][a-zA-Z0-9]+)*(?<! )$")
  @minLength(4)
  @maxLength(64)
  displayName: string;
```

ground_truth:
it is usually simpler and more explicit to add maxLength and minLength constraints to the custom scalar, like this, rather than using the pattern decorator
```
@maxLength(1)
@minLength(1)
scalar char extends string;
```

It is strongly recommended that property names and other types that become identifiers in generated SDKs not contain characters like underscore, because these are not easily representable as identifiers in all languages and/or violate the preferred coding style in some languages.  If you can, it is best to not use characters outside the standard set.  

If there is some compelling business reason not to use these characters, then it's possible to suppress the violation in typespec using an inline suppression, i.e.:

```
model Example {
  #suppress "@azure-tools/typespec-azure-core/casing-style" "<good-business-justification-here>
  priorityValue8021Action_Cluster: string;
}
```

query:
title: Can required fields have default values?

question: Does typespec allow default value for required field? I ask as mandatory field states that users send a value but we want to be able to assign a default for them. The field is below. 
```
model StorageDiscoveryWorkspaceProperties {
  @doc("The storage discovery sku.")
  @renamedFrom(ApiVersion.v2025_03_01_preview, "tier")
  @madeRequired(ApiVersion.v2025_06_01_preview)
  sku: StorageDiscoverySku;
```

ground_truth:
According to Azure ARM and REST API guidelines, properties that are patchable (i.e., those included in PATCH operations, typically with Lifecycle.Update visibility) should NOT have default values in the PATCH request schema. This is to ensure that PATCH only updates explicitly provided fields and does not unintentionally overwrite values with defaults.
However, if a property has a default value in the service (i.e., the service will assign a value if none is provided), the API specification (OpenAPI/TypeSpec) should document that default value for PUT/POST operations and in the resource schema, so that clients know what value will be used if they omit the property. 
In TypeSpec, you can set a default value for a property using the `=` syntax, e.g.:

```
model Example {
  value?: int32 = 42;
}
```

This will emit a `default` field in the OpenAPI schema for PUT/POST (create/update) operations and in the resource definition. But for PATCH operations, you must ensure that the PATCH schema does NOT include default values for patchable properties. The recommended approach is to use a separate PATCH model or a transformation (such as `Update<T>`) that omits defaults for PATCH requests.
For example, for a resource property with Lifecycle.Create, Lifecycle.Update, and Lifecycle.Read visibility:

```
model ResourceProperties {
  @visibility(Lifecycle.Create, Lifecycle.Update, Lifecycle.Read)
  myField?: int32 = 42; // Default value for PUT/POST, but NOT for PATCH
}

// PATCH model should omit default:
model ResourcePropertiesPatch is Update<ResourceProperties>;
```

Summary:
You CAN set a default value for a property with Lifecycle.Create/Update/Read, but the PATCH schema must NOT include the default value.
The OpenAPI spec should show the default value for PUT/POST and resource schemas if the service assigns one.
For PATCH, use a model or transformation that omits defaults, complying with ARM guidelines.

query:
title: Using namespaces and encountering duplicate-symbol error

question: I'm trying to decorate some types with a namespace, but I'm getting a duplicate-symbol error. Is there something wrong with the usage in the example below? I don't have any other usages of `namespace Chat` or `namespace OpenAI.Chat`.
```
